# Rank 1313 / CVE-2022-4070 (GHSA-x93j-3hh3-6x23, CWE-613, "Insufficient
# Session Expiration"): disabling a LibreNMS account revoked nothing that was
# already issued to it. Only the interactive credential path ever consulted
# the `enabled` column -- `LibreNMS\Authentication\MysqlAuthorizer::authenticate()`
# throws `AuthenticationException` when `$user_data->enabled` is false, and it
# is reached only through `App\Providers\LegacyUserProvider::validateCredentials()`,
# i.e. only when a password is actually being checked. Laravel's session guard
# restores an already-authenticated user through `retrieveById()` (a bare
# `User::find($identifier)`) and restores a "remember me" cookie holder through
# `retrieveByToken()`, which returns the user once the token compares equal and
# the username still exists in the auth backend:
#
#   public function retrieveByToken($identifier, $token)
#   {
#       $user = new User();
#       $user = $user->where($user->getAuthIdentifierName(), $identifier)->first();
#       if (! $user) { return null; }
#       $rememberToken = $user->getRememberToken();
#       if ($rememberToken && hash_equals($rememberToken, $token)) {
#           if (LegacyAuth::get()->userExists($user->username)) { return $user; }
#       }
#       return null;
#   }
#
# Neither path looks at `enabled`, so a live session cookie and a remember-me
# cookie both kept working indefinitely after an administrator disabled the
# account. The disabling side does not clean up either: `UserController::update()`
# explicitly revokes other sessions when the *password* changes
# (`Auth::logoutOtherDevices($request->new_password)`) and then applies every
# other validated field -- `enabled` among them, it is in `User::$fillable` and
# `UpdateUserRequest` validates it as `nullable` -- through
# `$user->fill($request->validated()); $user->save();` with no revocation at
# all.
#
# Pool commit ce8e5f3d05 is the real fix (metadata COMMIT_OK) and it closes the
# gap at neither of those places: it adds `App\Http\Middleware\VerifyUserEnabled`
# and registers it in `App\Http\Kernel`'s global `web` middleware group, so
# every web request re-checks `Auth::user()->enabled` after the session has been
# started and logs the account out when it is false. The rest of the commit is
# the user-facing half of the same change: the `enabled` control in
# `resources/views/user/form.blade.php` stops being gated on
# `auth_mechanism == 'mysql'` (so non-MySQL auth mechanisms can disable users at
# all), its hidden-input fallback's missing `@else` is corrected, and the login
# view learns to show the new `auth.disabled` message.
#
# ATTENTION, vocabulary gap. Scanning the vulnerable `app/` tree reports 179
# findings and none of them are this: `vyql match` on `app/Http/Kernel.php`
# labels nothing at all -- no source, no sink, no control -- and on
# `UserController::update` labels only the generic `code.MassAssign` at
# `$user->fill($request->validated())` and `code.StateChangingOp(Sink)` at
# `$user->save()`, which is VYQL-RF-043 firing identically on every controller
# write in the repository and on both revisions. The nearest shipped concept is
# `code.SessionHashMissingAuthorizationState` (VYQL-AUTH-058, CWE-613/CWE-863,
# "session validity hashes omit authorization state that can change while a
# session remains active"), which is the right weakness class but the wrong
# mechanism: it requires an authentication routine that stores a session
# validity hash derived from the password, and LibreNMS has no such hash -- the
# session is Laravel's own and the recaller is a remember-me token, so there is
# nothing for a hash-shaped binding to observe.
#
# No binding was written, because every form expressible here is either
# unsound or fires on the safe majority:
#
#   * The construct the fix actually changes is an *absence in a different
#     file*. `app/Http/Kernel.php`'s `web` group is where the account-status
#     gate is missing, but Kernel.php contains no evidence that this
#     application has an account-status attribute at all -- that lives in
#     `app/Models/User.php` (`$fillable` includes `enabled`),
#     `app/Http/Requests/UpdateUserRequest.php` (`'enabled' => 'nullable'`) and
#     `LibreNMS/Authentication/MysqlAuthorizer.php`. A binding is compiled
#     against per-file presence contexts (`node.scope == "module"` /
#     `"function"` / `"class"`), so "this app has a disable-user feature *and*
#     no middleware re-checks it" cannot be stated. Without the first half, the
#     binding reduces to "a Laravel web middleware group runs StartSession /
#     AuthenticateSession and no account-status middleware", which is true of
#     essentially every Laravel application, the overwhelming majority of which
#     have no account-status flag to check.
#
#   * Anchoring on `retrieveByToken()` instead -- a custom
#     `Illuminate\Contracts\Auth\UserProvider` whose remember-me lookup returns
#     the user on token equality alone -- is expressible and would fire on the
#     vulnerable file, but it does not discriminate the fix: upstream never
#     changed `retrieveByToken()`, and today's `LegacyUserProvider` still
#     performs exactly the same three checks. A rule that reports the fixed
#     revision as loudly as the vulnerable one is a false positive by the
#     project's own standard.
#
#   * Anchoring on the disabling side -- "a handler writes a user
#     account-status attribute and persists it with no session or remember-token
#     revocation" -- is the textbook CWE-613 statement and the shape ordinary
#     admin user-management CRUD has everywhere; re-checking status per request,
#     which is what LibreNMS chose, is a perfectly correct alternative to
#     revoking on change, so the rule would flag the correct design as well as
#     the broken one. It also cannot even see the account-status field here:
#     `UserController::update` names no attribute, it mass-assigns
#     `$request->validated()`.
#
# What would close this class is a concept for "a security-relevant account
# state (disabled, locked, role downgrade) that an authentication path checks
# only while verifying credentials, so already-issued sessions and persistent
# login tokens outlive the change", plus the ability for a binding to require
# evidence from more than one file of the same application -- the same
# whole-application predicate rank 1077 needed for a different reason. Both are
# design work rather than a bounded binding change, so nothing is shipped here.
#
# The four snippets below are the real code at both revisions. All four reject
# VYQL-AUTH-058, the nearest rule in the weakness class, and they pin the
# current behaviour so a future concept for this class has the pair waiting.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions reject
# VYQL-AUTH-058, the nearest rule in this weakness class.

test "rank1313: the web middleware group starts an authenticated session with no account-status re-check"
  lang php
  reject VYQL-AUTH-058
  file app/Http/Kernel.php
  ```
  <?php

  namespace App\Http;

  use Illuminate\Foundation\Http\Kernel as HttpKernel;

  class Kernel extends HttpKernel
  {
      protected $middlewareGroups = [
          'web' => [
              \App\Http\Middleware\CheckInstalled::class,
              \App\Http\Middleware\EncryptCookies::class,
              \Illuminate\Cookie\Middleware\AddQueuedCookiesToResponse::class,
              \Illuminate\Session\Middleware\StartSession::class,
              \Illuminate\Session\Middleware\AuthenticateSession::class,
              \Illuminate\View\Middleware\ShareErrorsFromSession::class,
              \App\Http\Middleware\VerifyCsrfToken::class,
              \Illuminate\Routing\Middleware\SubstituteBindings::class,
          ],

          'auth' => [
              \App\Http\Middleware\LegacyExternalAuth::class,
              'authenticate',
              \App\Http\Middleware\VerifyTwoFactor::class,
              \App\Http\Middleware\LoadUserPreferences::class,
          ],
      ];
  }
  ```
  file app/Providers/LegacyUserProvider.php
  ```
  <?php

  namespace App\Providers;

  use App\Models\User;
  use Illuminate\Contracts\Auth\Authenticatable;
  use Illuminate\Contracts\Auth\UserProvider;
  use LibreNMS\Authentication\LegacyAuth;
  use LibreNMS\Exceptions\AuthenticationException;

  class LegacyUserProvider implements UserProvider
  {
      public function retrieveById($identifier)
      {
          return User::find($identifier);
      }

      public function retrieveByToken($identifier, $token)
      {
          $user = new User();
          $user = $user->where($user->getAuthIdentifierName(), $identifier)->first();

          if (! $user) {
              return null;
          }

          $rememberToken = $user->getRememberToken();
          if ($rememberToken && hash_equals($rememberToken, $token)) {
              if (LegacyAuth::get()->userExists($user->username)) {
                  return $user;
              }
          }

          return null;
      }

      public function validateCredentials(Authenticatable $user, array $credentials)
      {
          $authorizer = LegacyAuth::get();

          try {
              if ($authorizer->authIsExternal()) {
                  $credentials['username'] = $authorizer->getExternalUsername();
              }

              if (empty($credentials['username']) || ! $authorizer->authenticate($credentials)) {
                  throw new AuthenticationException();
              }

              return true;
          } catch (AuthenticationException $ae) {
              return false;
          }
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1313: only the credential path consults the enabled column"
  lang php
  reject VYQL-AUTH-058
  file LibreNMS/Authentication/MysqlAuthorizer.php
  ```
  <?php

  namespace LibreNMS\Authentication;

  use App\Models\User;
  use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Hash;
  use LibreNMS\Exceptions\AuthenticationException;

  class MysqlAuthorizer extends AuthorizerBase
  {
      public function authenticate($credentials)
      {
          $username = $credentials['username'] ?? null;
          $password = $credentials['password'] ?? null;

          $user_data = User::whereNotNull('password')->firstWhere(['username' => $username]);
          $hash = $user_data->password;
          $enabled = $user_data->enabled;

          if (! $enabled) {
              throw new AuthenticationException();
          }

          if (Hash::check($password, $hash)) {
              if (Hash::needsRehash($hash)) {
                  $user_data->setPassword($password);
                  $user_data->save();
              }

              return true;
          }

          throw new AuthenticationException();
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1313: the update handler revokes sessions for a password change but not for the enabled flag"
  lang php
  reject VYQL-AUTH-058
  file app/Http/Controllers/UserController.php
  ```
  <?php

  namespace App\Http\Controllers;

  use App\Http\Requests\UpdateUserRequest;
  use App\Models\User;
  use Auth;
  use Flasher\Prime\FlasherInterface;

  class UserController extends Controller
  {
      public function update(UpdateUserRequest $request, User $user, FlasherInterface $flasher)
      {
          if ($request->get('new_password') && $user->canSetPassword($request->user())) {
              $user->setPassword($request->new_password);
              /** @var User $current_user */
              $current_user = Auth::user();
              Auth::setUser($user); // make sure new password is loaded, can only logout other sessions for the active user
              Auth::logoutOtherDevices($request->new_password);

              // when setting the password on another account, restore back to the user's account.
              if ($current_user->user_id !== $user->user_id) {
                  Auth::setUser($current_user);
              }
          }

          $user->fill($request->validated());

          if ($user->save()) {
              $flasher->addSuccess(__('User :username updated', ['username' => $user->username]));

              return redirect(route('users.index'));
          }

          $flasher->addError(__('Failed to update user :username', ['username' => $user->username]));

          return redirect()->back();
      }
  }
  ```

